WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.400
Glad you're here. Welcome to Models and Agents, episode 120. It's July 23, 2026.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:12.400
Their signal and noise in AI every day. Let's get to the signal.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:18.980
Anthropics new Cloud Security plugin turns your terminal into a multi-agent vulnerability scanner

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:25.940
that proposes patches you still review and apply. Anthropic released the Cloud Security plugin for

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:33.000
Cloud Code in beta. The tool runs a multi-agent vulnerability scan of a repository from inside an

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:39.360
existing Cloud Code session. It converts selected findings into patch files for manual review.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:44.840
The plugin operates directly in the terminal without requiring separate infrastructure.

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:51.020
Builders working on internal code bases now have an integrated way to surface issues before deployment.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:56.300
The announcement highlights its ability to scan and propose fixes without leaving the

00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:04.140
Cloud Code environment. This development continues the tracked agents and tool use arc from episode 119

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:10.460
by extending multi-turn agent reliability into security workflows. Watch for how the plugin

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:16.640
handles larger repositories and whether Anthropic expands the set of supported vulnerability classes

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:25.760
in follow-up updates. A new Rust-BPE tokenizer called Gigatoken hits 24.53 gigabytes per second on

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:35.280
144 core hardware. A detailed cost study across GPT, Claude, Gemini, and Kimmy shows real task costs

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:42.980
varying 10.6 times despite only two times published price differences. Poolside's small research team

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:50.720
trained Laguna S, a 118 billion mixture of experts model that beats think he's roughly one trillion

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:56.920
parameter open weights model through efficient infrastructure. Researchers released a session layer

00:01:56.920 --> 00:02:03.420
conversational risk accumulation framework that tracks semantic drift across multi-turn dialogues.

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:12.120
PiPI now rejects new file uploads to releases older than 14 days. A new paper studies hyper networks for

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:17.920
train time knowledge injection using a data set of tens of millions of multi-hop question answering

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:25.240
examples across 39 domains. The Claude security plugin integrates directly into existing Claude

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:31.860
code sessions. Users can trigger multi-agent scans and receive patch files without switching tools.

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:39.620
The beta focuses on terminal-based operation for immediate workflow fit. This builds on prior agent

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:45.600
tool use coverage by adding concrete security scanning inside the same session. Gigatoken

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:52.820
achieves its speed through a handwritten swar pre tokenizer and pre token caching. The implementation runs

00:02:52.820 --> 00:03:01.480
989 times faster than hugging face tokenizers on the same GPT2 workload. The gains come specifically

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:07.300
from the pre tokenizer and caching strategy rather than changes to the BPE merge loop.

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:12.680
Teams handling large scale pre-processing should test it for throughput gains.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:18.760
PoolSides model factory shows how smaller teams can close gaps through process improvements.

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:26.000
The co-CEO Esokant discussed construction of the factory capable of producing competitive models

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:33.740
at this size. This continues the tracked open weight models story from Episode 119 by demonstrating

00:03:33.740 --> 00:03:39.920
training efficiency gains. Watch for released details on the next model iteration as the factory

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:47.820
scales further. The real task cost benchmark ran 10 realistic product tasks across providers using

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:55.000
each cost optimized tier. Total costs spread 10.6 times even though published rates differed by

00:03:55.000 --> 00:04:02.080
only two times. One model used 197 reasoning tokens to produce a single word classification

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:10.300
answer. The study references cost bench from ACL 2026 and terminal world findings on failed agent

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:18.200
attempts burning more tokens. This data fits into the tracked reasoning models arc from Episode 119 by

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:24.760
highlighting invisible token costs that affect real world budgeting. The full methodology and 10

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:32.480
task prompts are public on GitHub at usewecker.com slash benchmark. The hyper network paper introduces

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:40.300
mega wiki qa constructed from wiki data 5m results show predictive power loss scaling along depth

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:47.800
width and target size axes plus reliable out of distribution generalization. The design decouples

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:54.040
the hyper networks injection capacity from the target models general capability. Teams exploring

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:59.120
efficient knowledge updates should examine the scaling curves for depth and width choices.

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:05.280
Researchers propose a conversational risk accumulation framework that tracks semantic drift,

00:05:05.980 --> 00:05:13.020
sensitivity weighted entity graphs and compliance gradients. The approach releases CRA bench with

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:22.500
1,208 turn sessions plus a five family extension reaching 2,000 sessions. Evaluation uses trajectory

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:31.480
AUROC turns to detection calibrated false positive metrics and leave one family out diagnostic stress tests.

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:39.100
This directly extends the tracked agents and tool use arc from Episode 119 by addressing gradual

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:45.700
intent drift that single turn guard rails miss. Pipi implemented the upload restriction after

00:05:45.700 --> 00:05:52.560
analysis showed no technical barrier had previously existed. Seth Larson noted that attacker simply had

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:59.180
not yet abused the prior openness. Maintainers of older releases should review their publishing workflows.

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:06.800
The restriction applies only to new files on existing older releases. The M diversity tool provides

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:12.120
a standardized set of embedding based diversity measures that work with any embedding model.

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:19.140
The package supports stylistic semantic language and speaker diversity calculations through a single

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:26.160
interface. Researchers can install it directly from the linked GitHub repository. The tool addresses

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:32.800
fragmentation of prior diversity measurement approaches. Install gigatoken and benchmark your current

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:38.920
pre-processing pipeline against hugging face tokenizers on large text corporate. Add the

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:45.260
Claude security plugin to an existing Claude code session and scan a non-production repository to see

00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:52.040
the patch workflow. Run the public 10 task, cost benchmark prompts against your top two providers to

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:59.260
measure real versus advertised spend. Explore the mega wiki QA dataset if you are testing hyper network

00:06:59.260 --> 00:07:06.200
or Laura based knowledge injection methods. Continued rollout of open AI presence enterprise agent

00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:12.940
features to additional eligible customers is expected. Further releases from pool sides model factory

00:07:12.940 --> 00:07:20.580
following Laguna S are on the way. Expanded CRA bench evaluations as more teams adopt the multi-turn

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:27.360
risk framework will appear soon. Additional scaling studies on hyper network knowledge injection as the

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:33.920
mega wiki QA results circulate are coming next. Okay, let's pop the hood on the invisible reasoning

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:41.380
tokens that drive the 10.6 times cost spread. Published per token prices suggest predictable spend.

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:48.640
In practice, many models generate substantial hidden reasoning traces, build at output rates but never

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:56.500
shown to the user. The Reddit benchmark revealed one model consuming 197 internal tokens to emit a

00:07:56.500 --> 00:08:03.360
single classification word. These traces arise because current reasoning training encourages step-by-step

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:10.640
computation even when the final answer is trivial. The effect compounds on agentic or multi-turn tasks

00:08:10.640 --> 00:08:17.620
where failed attempts burn extra tokens at high rates. This matches the reported negative 0.62

00:08:17.620 --> 00:08:24.380
correlation between failed attempts and token burn. When choosing providers, run your actual workload

00:08:24.380 --> 00:08:31.520
rather than relying on list prices. The practical decision rule is to measure end-to-end token usage on

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:38.500
representative tasks before committing to a cost-optimized tier. Tomorrow keep an eye on further pool

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:45.660
side model factory updates and expanded CRA bench results. That's models and agents for today.

